00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:14.040
right? So the motivation for this work, as well&nbsp;
as most of my work, is to support decision making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:20.000
in engineering and science. And when we think&nbsp;
about the decisions that we might want to make,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.120
a lot of times, they rely on what I will call a&nbsp;
many query computation. So that includes things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:31.560
like optimization, uncertainty quantification&nbsp;
and control tasks. So in optimization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:37.840
you might have to query a model at every step of&nbsp;
a gradient based iterative method. In uncertainty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.760
quantification, you would be querying a model&nbsp;
for every sample of say, like a Monte Carlo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:47.040
like uncertainty quantification, computation&nbsp;
and in control computations, you're querying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.360
the model. Basically you know at every second&nbsp;
say in real time to understand, like, what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.360 --> 00:05:00.320
next control input should be. So traditional&nbsp;
numerical simulations that comprise. Most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.080
our traditional models are based on physics.&nbsp;
They're often high dimensional because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:12.480
result from discretizing a continuous governing&nbsp;
equation on a high dimensional computational mesh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:18.600
and that makes them generally too expensive for&nbsp;
this many query setting. So these, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:23.040
you're not going to be solving these traditional&nbsp;
high dimensional simulations, you know, in real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:28.880
time, say, for control, or you can't afford to do&nbsp;
that many samples in uncertainty quantification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.120
So there's a real need for efficient, you know.&nbsp;
And when I say efficient, I'm thinking like fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:41.800
so computationally efficient and cheap surrogate&nbsp;
models to enable decision making. So I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:45.920
everybody would probably agree that there's been&nbsp;
a lot of interest in machine learning methods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:54.520
for surrogate modeling, for engineering systems,&nbsp;
they've shown a lot of promise, partially through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.520 --> 00:06:00.160
some very visible successes in other domains that&nbsp;
were traditionally seen as extremely challenging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.160
so in computer vision and natural language&nbsp;
processing, and, say, playing, playing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:10.360
game of Go. So they've seen some real practical&nbsp;
successes. And in addition to those practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:16.240
successes, there are some theoretical guarantees&nbsp;
of of what I call the expressive power of these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.120
of these machine learning models. So I'm thinking&nbsp;
in particular about neural networks, for which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:25.160
there are a lot of theoretical guarantees that,&nbsp;
say, like, there is a neural network of some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:30.320
shape and size and form with some parameters that&nbsp;
can match, like, you know, sort of any complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:37.680
nonlinear function, like, perfectly, essentially.&nbsp;
But there's a significant gap between that theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:43.200
and engineering practice, and that's because&nbsp;
of, like, basically what I was saying, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.560
there is a neural network that can match things&nbsp;
perfectly. It's just we don't know a lot about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:51.000
like, what the neural network is like, what its&nbsp;
architecture is, what its parameters should be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:57.320
And so because there's that gap between the theory&nbsp;
and the practice, there's a real lack of trust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:03.560
I think, for using these machine learned&nbsp;
models for engineering design, where you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:08.480
some consequences, that impact, that have, like,&nbsp;
you know, high costs, both in terms of dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:15.920
but also in terms of, like, potentially human&nbsp;
safety. And so that's a significant barrier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:22.640
to the widespread adoption of machine learning&nbsp;
type methods in engineering. So to address that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:29.520
one might ask, what is the reason that we have&nbsp;
trust in traditional numerical simulations? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.080
I would say that some of the reasons for trust in&nbsp;
traditional numerical simulations are that they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:37.840
based in physics, which is something that we sort&nbsp;
of understand to be true. So there's a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.640
foundation. Additionally, we make some really&nbsp;
explicit and transparent modeling assumptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:46.560
in physics based simulations. So for example,&nbsp;
like, if you're going to simulate a fluid flow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.440
with Euler versus Navier Stokes, like, you&nbsp;
know that you're making an assumption in Euler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:57.640
equations that, like, the viscous effects are&nbsp;
negligible. And that helps you understand both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:01.320
when to trust the simulations as well as when&nbsp;
we don't. And in addition, we have, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:07.800
many decades of numerical analysis that help us&nbsp;
understand the errors in these discretized these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:15.240
like, sort of discretized PDE models. So&nbsp;
then we ask, okay, so in machine learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:23.320
or in statistical learning, like, where do we&nbsp;
get trust from? And I would in sort of many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:28.160
these applications. Trust comes from big data,&nbsp;
like the idea that you have so much data that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:32.600
like, you've kind of, like seen everything&nbsp;
there is to see, and you can really represent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:40.720
like that reality quite accurately. Okay, so to&nbsp;
be more mathematically specific about what that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:46.040
trust means. I'm going to say, Okay, we're going&nbsp;
to consider an input random variable z and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:55.760
output random variable y1 and y1 is some function&nbsp;
f1 of z. And in the setting that I'm considering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

